        "caption": {
          "en": "Enlargement with fractional scale factor \\(k = 0.5\\): all linear dimensions are halved, and the area is scaled by \\(k^2 = 0.25\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠️ Cambridge 0580 Traps — Transformations & Vectors",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Single Transformation Rule:</strong> Examination instructions strictly forbid giving two transformations to describe a mapping. Stating 'reflection then translation' scores 0 marks even if both descriptions are correct. You must identify the single transformation: Translation, Reflection, Rotation, or Enlargement.</p><p><strong>Required Transformation Details:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Translation:</strong> state column vector \\(\\binom{x}{y}\\).</li><li><strong>Reflection:</strong> state equation of mirror line (e.g. \\(y = x\\) or \\(x = 2\\)).</li><li><strong>Rotation:</strong> state angle (clockwise/anticlockwise) AND centre of rotation \\((h, k)\\).</li><li><strong>Enlargement:</strong> state scale factor \\(k\\) AND centre of enlargement \\((h, k)\\).</li></ul><p><strong>Negative Enlargement:</strong> Scale factor \\(k = -2\\) produces an inverted shape on the <em>opposite side</em> of the centre of enlargement at twice the distance.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "In triangle \\(OAB\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} = 3\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OB} = 6\\mathbf{b}\\). Point \\(P\\) lies on \\(AB\\) such that \\(AP : PB = 1 : 2\\). Find \\(\\overrightarrow{OP}\\) in terms of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) in its simplest form.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Step 1: Express \\(\\overrightarrow{AB}\\):</strong></p>\\[\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\overrightarrow{AO} + \\overrightarrow{OB} = -3\\mathbf{a} + 6\\mathbf{b}\\]<p><strong>Step 2: Find \\(\\overrightarrow{AP}\\):</strong></p>\\[\\overrightarrow{AP} = \\frac{1}{3}\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\frac{1}{3}(-3\\mathbf{a} + 6\\mathbf{b}) = -\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}\\]<p><strong>Step 3: Find \\(\\overrightarrow{OP}\\):</strong></p>\\[\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\overrightarrow{OA} + \\overrightarrow{AP} = 3\\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) = 2\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "[Transformations &amp; Vectors · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is \\( g(t) = 5t + 5 \\), evaluate \\( g(8) \\) and find the inverse input \\( t \\) when \\( g(t) = 50 \\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Substitute \\( t = 8 \\): \\( g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45} \\). Setting \\( 5t + 5 = 50 \\) gives \\( 5t = 45 \\implies t = \\mathbf{9} \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "[Transformations &amp; Vectors · Practice 4] If the governing relation for this system is \\( g(t) = 6t + 6 \\), evaluate \\( g(10) \\) and find the inverse input \\( t \\) when \\( g(t) = 72 \\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Substitute \\( t = 10 \\): \\( g(10) = 6(10) + 6 = \\mathbf{66} \\). Setting \\( 6t + 6 = 72 \\) gives \\( 6t = 66 \\implies t = \\mathbf{11} \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
